  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1237 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nBy Monday night the eastern 500 MB trough will have shifted \nover Quebec, while the western one crosses from Saskatchewan \ninto Manitoba, then subsequently shifts east southeastward into \nthe Lake Superior region by Wednesday. In Iowa this will be \nconcurrent with a very light surface pressure/flow pattern, \nresulting in nebulous low rain chances at times from Monday \nnight into Wednesday. As the 500 MB trough moves over Lake \nSuperior, it will help push a large surface high pressure area \ndown the northern High Plains and Midwest during this time, \nlater bringing relatively comfortable and dry weather from \naround Wednesday night through Friday. The onset of the \ninfluence of this surface ridge will take the form of a modest \ntrough, pushing through ahead of the main mid-level shortwave \nsometime around Tuesday night. Differences in timing and the \nsharpness of the trough lead to a high variability in model \nsolutions, however, there does appear to be some potential for \ngood destabilization on Tuesday afternoon/evening and also \npotential for moderate deep-layer (0-6 KM) shear of perhaps \n30-35 KT or so. Again, there is significant variability in \nthese solutions and much will depend on diurnal timing, but it \nis possible if the trough has enough low-level convergence and \ncomes through during peak diurnal heating time, there may be a \nnarrow window of opportunity for pulse storms with a severe \nweather threat late Tuesday. A more likely scenario, however, \nis that the trough comes through overnight with much less \ninstability and/or weaker shear, or that the trough is too weak \nand low-level convergence is not sufficient to support stronger \nupdrafts, or both. By next weekend, the 500 MB pattern should \nbegin to shift just a bit, with the ridge flattening and \nshifting a bit toward the southwestern U.S., while a more zonal \nflow pattern sets up across the northern CONUS and Iowa. This \nwould favor a somewhat more active weather regime in our \nregion, with persistent rain chances likely returning. Of \ncourse that is still a week-plus out, but something to keep an \neye on in the coming days.",
